## Bug Identification

**Line 2:** The condition `input !== null && input !== undefined && input !== false` does not account for the empty string `""`.

In JavaScript, `""` (empty string) is **not** equal to `null`, `undefined`, or `false` via strict inequality (`!==`). It's its own distinct value. So all three comparisons evaluate to `true`, the `if` body executes, and the function returns `true` — which is the opposite of the intended behavior.

## Fix

Add an explicit check for the empty string to the condition:

```javascript
function validateInput(input) {
    if (input !== null && input !== undefined && input !== false && input !== "") {
        return true;
    }
    return false;
}
```

Now `validateInput("")` will hit the new `input !== ""` guard (which is `false`), short-circuit the `&&` chain, skip the `return true`, and fall through to `return false`.

<solution language="javascript" verdict="fix">
function validateInput(input) {
    if (input !== null && input !== undefined && input !== false && input !== "") {
        return true;
    }
    return false;
}
</solution>

