## The Bug

The problem is on this line inside the loop:

```go
go func() {
    defer wg.Done()
    fmt.Println(i)  // ← captures the loop variable by reference
}()
```

In Go 1.21, the loop variable `i` is a **single variable shared across all iterations**. The anonymous function doesn't get its own copy — it closes over the same `i`. By the time the goroutines are actually scheduled to run, the `for` loop has already finished and `i` is `5`. So every goroutine prints `5`.

## The Fix

Pass `i` as a parameter to the goroutine so each one captures its own copy of the value:

```go
go func(i int) {
    defer wg.Done()
    fmt.Println(i)
}(i)
```

> **Side note:** Starting with Go 1.22, the loop variable semantics changed so that each iteration creates a new variable. If you upgrade, the original code will work as expected. But on Go 1.21 you must use the explicit parameter (or a `i := i` local copy).

<solution language="go" verdict="fix">
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sync"
)

func main() {
	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
		wg.Add(1)
		go func(i int) {
			defer wg.Done()
			fmt.Println(i)
		}(i)
	}
	wg.Wait()
}
</solution>

